st either way.\n  `--no-reasoning-replay` / `{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` opts out.\n- Put the developer in control of capture, sharing, and deletion. Sharing is\n  a deliberate choice, not a side effect: today it is an explicit\n  `cledger sync`; the decided direction (see Conversation Ledger's design\n  doc) is optional-but-default-on transport hooks so records ride normal\n  `git push`/`pull`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n  user. Listings default to the current user's records.\n\n## Non-goals\n\n- A team presence system, task tracker, or agent orchestration framework.\n- Inferring or replacing human intent.\n- Summarized \"handoff notes\" (sticky-note-style); Turnbridge moves the literal\n  record.\n\n## Relationship to Conversation Ledger\n\nTurnbridge is a client of Conversation Ledger, which owns the durable neutral\nrecord **and** the source-CLI capture adapters (hooks, transcript parsers,\ncursors, dedup). Turnbridge owns everything on the resume side: the unified\npicker, ownership/compatibility filtering, target-CLI session fabrication,\nbootstrap rehydration, and the optional command shims.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md","numLines":57,"startLine":1,"totalLines":57},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"d5fa89ee-4167-49af-abee-fe9592815950","version":"2.1.228"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-12T01:24:34.427Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# Product intent\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** design-stage impressions. Nothing here is committed; it exists to\n4\tframe the problem and record early opinions, and is expected to shift. The\n5\thonest framing of the whole project is not \"a known use to build toward\" but\n6\t\"a general structure with good reason to expect multiple, extremely diverse\n7\tdownstream consumers.\"\n8\t\n9\t## Purpose\n10\t\n11\tContext Graph makes the relationships *among a repository's artifacts*\n12\texplicit and addressable. Code, docs, conversations, and commits are all\n13\tpartial expressions of the same work, densely connected — a file imports\n14\tanother, a conversation touches a file, a commit modifies it — but those\n15\tconnections are never recorded as first-class structure. This tool derives\n16\tthem into one typed graph.\n17\t\n18\tThe output is a **data structure**, not an application. Downstream consumers\n19\tdecide what it is for:\n20\t\n21\t- an LLM agent traversing it to help a human with a task (a human is not\n22\t  expected to navigate it directly, and no human-facing UX is planned);\n23\t- training pipelines for future experiments and productionized versions of\n24\t  tagseq2tagseq-like models, which learn by traversing text-attributed\n25\t  graphs;\n26\t- possibly intent-recall, if it turns out to need graph structure rather than\n27\t  raw ledger access.\n28\t\n29\tTraversal tooling itself — agent-facing query tools, a\n30\t`torch.utils.data.Dataset`, exporters — may well live downstream of this\n31\trepo. Context Graph's job ends at producing the structure.\n32\t\n33\t## Long-term vision\n34\t\n35\tThe hope is that this and its downstream consumers become useful enough that\n36\tpeople willingly install it into their repositories — for use by their agents\n37\tday to day, and to willingly contribute their data to downstream training\n38\tinfrastructure (privacy-preserving federated learning is one imagined shape).\n39\tThe same derivation should scale across scopes: one repository; all of one\n40\tperson's repositories as a single cross-repo graph; many people's\n41\trepositories as a cross-repo, cross-person graph.\n42\t\n43\t## Behavioral commitments (intended)\n44\t\n45\t- Derive edges from evidence that already exists (ledger events, import\n46\t  statements, doc links, commit metadata); never invent a relationship the\n47\t  artifacts do not support.\n48\t- Every edge carries its basis — a pointer to the events, lines, or commits\n49\t  that justify it. There are no confidence scores and no model-judged edges:\n50\t  extraction is mechanical, and an edge's kind plus its basis *is* the trust\n51\t  signal. Semantic inference over the record is downstream work (plausibly\n52\t  intent-recall's), not this tool's.\n53\t- The graph is a derived, rebuildable view computed live from the repository's\n54\t  git history and its Conversation Ledger. Anything persisted is a cache, a\n55\t  pure optimization; the underlying artifacts stay authoritative and\n56\t  re-deriving from the same inputs yields the same graph.\n57\t- Distinguish edge kinds rather than flattening everything into one \"related\"\n58\t  link.\n59\t- The graph is a whole-history structure: it reads *all* users' ledger\n60\t  records, not just the current user's. (Turnbridge's own-conversations-only\n61\t  listing is a UX default against confusion, not a boundary; the ledger\n62\t  already gates secrets at capture/transport.)\n63\t\n64\t## Non-goals (for now)\n65\t\n66\t- Deciding which artifact is \"correct,\" ranking intent, or any judgment over\n67\t  the record — downstream consumers' job.\n68\t- Capturing conversations or moving them between CLIs — turnbridge and\n69\t  conversation-ledger.\n70\t- A second store: no new storage system. Nodes reference artifacts; content\n71\t  lives where it already lives (working tree, git objects, ledger notes).\n72\t- Committing to any consumer-specific export format (`graph.jsonl` included).\n73\t  Exporters are downstream concerns.\n74\t- A human-facing navigation UI.\n75\t\n76\t## Relationship to the other tools\n77\t\n78\tContext Graph is a **reader** of git history and Conversation Ledger, and a\n79\t**producer** of structure for downstream consumers. turnbridge already emits\n80\t`continuation` lineage links into the ledger; this tool is the first that\n81\treads them as graph structure rather than as per-row labels.\n82\t\n83\ttagseq2tagseq is a nearly finished MVP research project and serves as\n84\tinspiration only — Context Graph will not integrate into its codebase. Its\n85\tsuccessors (future experiments, productionized descendants) are the natural\n86\ttraining-side consumers.\n87\t\n88\tintent-recall is itself at a pre-requirements design stage; nothing about it\n89\tshould be assumed, including its own design docs. It may remain a parallel\n90\treader of the ledger, or it may come to depend on this graph. Undecided.\n91\t\n92\t## Scope and sequencing\n93\t\n94\tConversations are a natural first artifact type to wire up, because the\n95\tledger already stores them with provenance and turnbridge already writes\n96\tlineage links. Code, doc, and commit extractors follow. That is a build\n97\torder, not a set of separate products; whether every stage lives in this repo\n98\tor some split into downstream siblings is undecided.\n99\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_01CDP6Yvsmj5rntuj2eN5REs","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"defab1d48ffb334ef077b543c74110f5a05efd71","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","seq":196},"id":"ev1-319e6d9f3b14aff5dab0207ea41a34d5634fca9de8430ac33a5dd1d82675fa91","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-14T01:54:42.931Z","producer":{"session_id":"60ec7095-