ingle cross-repo graph; many people's\nrepositories as a cross-repo, cross-person graph.\n\n## Behavioral commitments (intended)\n\n- Derive edges from evidence that already exists (ledger events, import\n  statements, doc links, commit metadata); never invent a relationship the\n  artifacts do not support.\n- Every edge carries its basis — a pointer to the events, lines, or commits\n  that justify it. There are no confidence scores and no model-judged edges:\n  extraction is mechanical, and an edge's kind plus its basis *is* the trust\n  signal. Semantic inference over the record is downstream work (plausibly\n  intent-recall's), not this tool's.\n- The graph is a derived, rebuildable view computed live from the repository's\n  git history and its Conversation Ledger. Anything persisted is a cache, a\n  pure optimization; the underlying artifacts stay authoritative and\n  re-deriving from the same inputs yields the same graph.\n- Distinguish edge kinds rather than flattening everything into one \"related\"\n  link.\n- The graph is a whole-history structure: it reads *all* users' ledger\n  records, not just the current user's. (Turnbridge's own-conversations-only\n  listing is a UX default against confusion, not a boundary; the ledger\n  already gates secrets at capture/transport.)\n\n## Non-goals (for now)\n\n- Deciding which artifact is \"correct,\" ranking intent, or any judgment over\n  the record — downstream consumers' job.\n- Capturing conversations or moving them between CLIs — turnbridge and\n  conversation-ledger.\n- A second store: no new storage system. Nodes reference artifacts; content\n  lives where it already lives (working tree, git objects, ledger notes).\n- Committing to any consumer-specific export format (`graph.jsonl` included).\n  Exporters are downstream concerns.\n- A human-facing navigation UI.\n\n## Relationship to the other tools\n\nContext Graph is a **reader** of git history and Conversation Ledger, and a\n**producer** of structure for downstream consumers. turnbridge already emits\n`continuation` lineage links into the ledger; this tool is the first that\nreads them as graph structure rather than as per-row labels.\n\ntagseq2tagseq is a nearly finished MVP research project and serves as\ninspiration only — Context Graph will not integrate into its codebase. Its\nsuccessors (future experiments, productionized descendants) are the natural\ntraining-side consumers.\n\nintent-recall is itself at a pre-requirements design stage; nothing about it\nshould be assumed, including its own design docs. It may remain a parallel\nreader of the ledger, or it may come to depend on this graph. Undecided.\n\n## Scope and sequencing\n\nConversations are a natural first artifact type to wire up, because the\nledger already stores them with provenance and turnbridge already writes\nlineage links. Code, doc, and commit extractors follow. That is a build\norder, not a set of separate products; whether every stage lives in this repo\nor some split into downstream siblings is undecided.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/context-graph/docs/PRODUCT_INTENT.md","numLines":99,"startLine":1,"totalLines":99},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"4d5c0402-f3ee-4921-9a56-3e41bf9f6fa9","version":"2.1.232"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-14T01:57:41.216Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# WIP technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** MVP implemented for Claude Code ⇄ Codex ⇄ opencode; fabrication\n4\tadapters are version-pinned and must be revalidated per CLI release.\n5\t\n6\t## Main flow\n7\t\n8\t1. Conversation Ledger's hooks (`cledger install all`) capture each CLI's\n9\t   local transcript incrementally into git notes as normalized\n10\t   `conversation_turn` events, stamped with the repo's git identity\n11\t   (`actor.id` = `user.email`) on human turns. opencode has no transcript file\n12\t   to tail — cledger captures it through a `session.idle` plugin that converts\n13\t   `opencode export`, one event per *part*, since its store is mutable\n14\t   (cledger 0.18.0+).\n15\t2. `turnbridge resume [claude|codex|opencode]` lists conversations compatible with the\n16\t   current repository state: events anchored to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n17\t   (`--any-commit` lifts this), authored by you or unattributed\n18\t   (`--all` includes collaborators).\n19\t3. Selecting a conversation whose source matches the target CLI delegates to\n20\t   that CLI's native resume.\n21\t4. Cross-CLI selections default to **native fabrication**: turnbridge writes a\n22\t   target-native session file from the canonical events and launches the\n23\t   target's own resume on it. `--bootstrap` (also the automatic fallback when\n24\t   fabrication is unsupported or fails validation) starts a fresh target\n25\t   session whose first prompt instructs it to read the literal transcript.\n26\t\n27\t## Module map\n28\t\n29\t- `src/conversations.ts` — ledger read + grouping + ownership/compat filter.\n30\t- `src/resume.ts` — picker flow, target choice, default-target config.\n31\t- `src/targets/*` — per-CLI `TargetAdapter`: `nativeResume`, `fabricate`,\n32\t  `bootstrap`. Fabrication throws `FabricationUnsupportedError` to trigger the\n33\t  fallback.\n34\t- `src/transcript.ts` + `src/bootstrap.ts` — literal markdown rendering and\n35\t  the rehydration prompt; reports estimated token size before launch.\n36\t- `src/shim.ts` — opt-in PATH shims: bare `claude --resume` / `codex resume`\n37\t  open the merged picker; everything else passes through to the real binary.\n38\t  No opencode shim: it has no argument-free \"show me my sessions\" invocation to\n39\t  interpose on (`-s` always takes an id), and intercepting one that takes an\n40\t  argument would both discard the id and recurse through the shim.\n41\t\n42\t## Division of labor with Conversation Ledger\n43\t\n44\tThe ledger owns capture (adapters, cursors, idempotent event ids, git-notes\n45\tstorage, transport). Turnbridge never parses source transcripts itself and\n46\tis a reader plus a writer of *target-native* session files. It writes exactly\n47\tone kind of ledger event — `continuation` lineage edges (below) — and no\n48\tconversation content. Ledger changes made for turnbridge: a library entry\n49\tpoint (`package.json` exports) and human-turn identity stamping.\n50\t\n51\tTurnbridge's reads inherit cledger 0.8.0's read-time behavior: `readEvents()`\n52\tlazily absorbs whatever transport already fetched into the local ref, then\n53\tauto-runs squash/rebase re-anchor detection, which can append a `re_anchor`\n54\tmapping event and print a notice to stderr. Reachability filtering resolves\n55\tthrough `re_anchor` mappings, so a bridged conversation and its `continuation`\n56\tlineage survive a squash merge instead of falling out of the picker. Turnbridge\n57\tissues two such reads per invocation (conversation listing, then lineage\n58\tlookup) and buffers the ledger's stderr notices across both so they render as\n59\tone block above the picker rather than interleaving with it.\n60\t\n61\t## Conversation lineage (branch-on-bridge)\n62\t\n63\tBridging is a fork, not a move. Fabricating a target session writes a real\n64\tnative file; when the user continues there, the target CLI captures it as a\n65\tnew conversation whose id is the session id turnbridge generated (verified:\n66\tCodex resume preserves the id and appends). The source conversation still\n67\texists, so both are resumable — a genuine branch, like a git fork.\n68\t\n69\tTo keep that honest instead of confusing, at fabricate time turnbridge appends\n70\ta `continuation` event (`links: [{rel:\"continues\", target:<source>}]`, content\n71\tcarries `imported_through_seq`) belonging to the target conversation. The\n72\tpicker reads these and labels rows in both directions (`→ bridged to Codex`,\n73\t`↳ continued from Claude Code`); re-bridging a conversation that already\n74\tembeds imported history is allowed but flagged. The default listing shows\n75\tboth branch endpoints — abandonment is not assumed. The same edge is what lets\n76\ta downstream consumer (intent-recall) de-duplicate the copied prefix: target\n77\tturns `0..imported_through_seq` are known copies of the source, not fresh\n78\tevidence, so turnbridge does not need to alter capture to avoid double-count.\n79\t\n80\t## Fabrication contract (per target adapter)\n81\t\n82\t**Governing rule: the fabricated session has to work in the target.** Fidelity\n83\tto the source is a property of a session that functions, not a substitute for\n84\tone. Where a target's native records demand a particular shape — a resolvable\n85\tprovider id, a specific part structure — match that shape, and carry the\n86\tsource's identity somewhere that does not break resume. A bridged session that\n87\tis unimpeachably honest and cannot be continued has failed at the only thing it\n88\texists to do. *(Corrected 2026-08-06, after `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` silently\n89\tbricked continuation on every bridged opencode session — see the opencode notes\n90\tbelow.)*\n91\t\n92\tEvery field a fabricated session carries falls into one of three tiers, and the\n93\ttier decides where the logic lives:\n94\t\n95\t1. **Portable** — the same meaning in every target: turn role, visible text,\n96\t   ordering, timestamps. Shared code writes these once; adapters do not touch\n97\t   them.\n98\t2. **Portable by default, overridden where a target requires it** — usually\n99\t   transferable, but some targets reject or mishandle the generic form, so the\n100\t   adapter substitutes a working value and discloses the substitution. The\n101\t   source model id is the type case; foreign tool calls are the other\n102\t   (structured where the target accepts unknown names, labeled text where not).\n103\t3. **Target-specific** — no portable form exists and the adapter owns it\n104\t   outright: opencode's `providerID`/`projectID` and its `step-start`/\n105\t   `step-finish` part scaffolding, Claude's `parentUuid` chain, Codex's paired\n106\t   `event_msg`/`response_item` twins.\n107\t\n108\tTiers 2 and 3 are what the adapter abstraction exists for. Discovering that a\n109\tfield assumed to be tier 1 is really tier 2 is a contract change worth\n110\trecording here, not a quiet workaround inside one adapter.\n111\t\n112\t- Pin the CLI versions the writer was validated against; on an unknown version\n113\t  warn and offer bootstrap.\n114\t- Write only new session files under the CLI's own session directory; never\n115\t  modify existing native sessions.\n116\t- Represent foreign tool calls faithfully (or as clearly-labeled text when the\n117\t  target rejects unknown tool schemas — see per-adapter spec notes).\n118\t- Propagate the source model id into fabricated assistant envelopes verbatim\n119\t  **where the target can resolve it** (decided 2026-07-21, amended 2026-08-06).\n120\t  If the target recognizes it — e.g. the user registered matching external\n121\t  models in Claude Code — the session restores seamlessly. If it does not, write\n122\t  a value the target *can* resolve and disclose the real source model in the\n123\t  import notice; do not leave an unresolvable id in a field the target uses to\n124\t  dispatch. The original rule (\"never substitute a recognized-but-false id\")\n125\t  was written to keep the transcript honest and instead produced sessions that\n126\t  could not be continued at all; honesty belongs in the disclosure, not in a\n127\t  dispatch field.\n128\t\n129\tReverse-engineered, empirically verified format specs (pinned versions,\n130\tminimal working schemas, unknowns): [docs/specs/claude-session-format.md](specs/claude-session-format.md),\n131\t[docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md](specs/codex-rollout-format.md), and\n132\t[docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md](specs/opencode-session-format.md).\n133\tKey constraints: Claude resume is scoped to the project dir encoded from the\n134\tlaunch cwd and reconstructs history by walking `parentUuid` from the last\n135\tline; Codex resume-by-id scans `~/.codex/sessions` for a matching filename +\n136\t`session_meta` id, and only plain `message` response_items are verified-safe\n137\tto replay (foreign tool calls fold into labeled text). Claude accepts foreign\n138\t`tool_use` names verbatim, so Codex→Claude keeps structured tool history.\n139\t\n140\topencode is the first target that breaks the \"write a new session file\"\n141\tassumption: every session lives in one shared SQLite DB, so fabrication builds\n142\ta payload in opencode's own export format and shells out to `opencode import`.\n143\tIt is also the target that taught the tier model its lesson twice over: the\n144\tsession's stored `providerID` is what its agent loop dispatches from (an\n145\tunresolvable id is fatal, not cosmetic), and **ids are an ordering key** —\n146\tparts render in `id` order, and message ids must sort ahead of the ones\n147\topencode mints next, or a resumed session answers and then keeps generating.\n148\tBoth are tier 3, and both looked fine on screen while being broken underneath.\n149\tThree further consequences the file-based targets don't have. Import is **not atomic**\n150\t— a payload rejected part-way leaves a truncated session behind, so a failed\n151\timport deletes it before falling back to bootstrap. Message order comes from\n152\t`time.created` rather than payload position, so the import notice is backdated\n153\tahead of history instead of stamped `now` (stamped `now`, it sorted last and\n154\trendered as a `QUEUED` prompt). And `info.projectID` — an opaque id looked up\n155\tvia `opencode debug scrap`, not derivable from the path — is what scopes a\n156\tsession to a repo; the `global` fallback lists it from every directory.\n157\t\n158\t## Sharing model\n159\t\n160\t`actor.id`/`actor.display` come from `git config user.email/name` at capture\n161\ttime. Default listings: own + unattributed conversations; `--all` shows every\n162\tauthor with labels. Transport is optional-but-default-on: Conversation\n163\tLedger's auto-installed pre-push hook and fetch refspec ride normal\n164\t`git push`/`git fetch`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n165\tuser (`{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}`); `cledger sync`\n166\tremains available for an explicit push/fetch/merge. Turnbridge adds no\n167\tnetwork behavior of its own — it only reads whatever the ledger has already\n168\tabsorbed locally. Events captured before identity stamping existed are\n169\tunattributed; a forced transcript rescan after the upgrade can duplicate\n170\tthose turns under new event ids.\n171\t\n172\t## Risks\n173\t\n174\tNative formats change without notice (fabrication is version-pinned, bootstrap\n175\tis the recovery path); transcript content can include secrets (the ledger\n176\tredacts at capture and sync time, but matching is pattern/entropy-based and\n177\tcannot catch every secret — review before sharing still matters); full\n178\thistories can exceed the target's context (size is reported before launch);\n179\tcrashes mid-turn leave partial capture, which the ledger tolerates by design.\n180\t\n181\t## Roadmap\n182\t\n183\tToward an npm release (decided 2026-07-21; deepen the two existing targets\n184\tbefore broadening):\n185\t\n186\t- Publish `conversation-ledger` to npm (name is unclaimed), then swap the\n187\t  `file:` dependency for a semver range — `prepublishOnly` refuses to publish\n188\t  until this happens. Deliberately deferred while both packages are iterated\n189\t  on and dogfooded for a few days.\n190\t- Adapter-drift automation stays **reporting-only** (decided 2026-08-02). The\n191\t  daily check (`.github/workflows/adapter-drift.yml`) opens a tracking issue on\n192\t  drift and now closes it when drift clears; it never writes to the repo.\n193\t  Auto-drafting the revalidation PR was built and then reverted. The reasoning\n194\t  is worth keeping, because the idea is tempting enough to recur: CI cannot\n195\t  validate a pin — the probes need both target CLIs installed, a TTY for the\n196\t  interactive smoke test, and paid model credentials per provider to make the\n197\t  recall turns real. So any PR CI opened would be a mechanical one-line bump\n198\t  with nothing behind it, saving a trivial edit while creating a *mergeable*\n199\t  artifact that asserts a validation nobody performed. An issue cannot be\n200\t  merged, and that asymmetry is the point. The agent-drafted variant (the old\n201\t  stage 2, needing an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret) fails for the same reason —\n202\t  the API key was never the binding constraint; the provider credentials the\n203\t  probes need are, and no amount of agency in CI conjures them.\n204\t- **opencode is a full member** *(target added 2026-08-02, opencode 1.18.5;\n205\t  capture landed in conversation-ledger 0.18.0 and verified here 2026-08-03)*.\n206\t  Both directions are exercised: a Claude Code conversation bridged into\n207\t  opencode is captured back by cledger's `session.idle` plugin, and bridging\n208\t  that opencode conversation onward into Codex and Claude Code preserves the\n209\t  tool call as a structured `function_call`/`function_call_output` pair and as\n210\t  `tool_use`/`tool_result` respectively, with the source model id intact.\n211\t  Note the asymmetry cledger's README calls out: opencode `reasoning` parts are\n212\t  plaintext, so they arrive as ordinary visible `thinking` blocks rather than\n213\t  as Codex-style opaque ciphertext — nothing here replays provider-private\n214\t  state for this target. Remaining opencode gaps: no headless model-recall\n215\t  probe (the Codex analogue is `probe-codex-content.mjs`), and long histories\n216\t  unmeasured — see the spec's \"Unknowns\".\n217\t- Revalidation tooling exists and is the bar for widening version pins:\n218\t  `npm run smoke:interactive` (real-TUI render check, all three targets),\n219\t  `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs` (headless model-recall probe),\n220\t  `probe-session-invariants.mjs` (malformed-session tolerance),\n221\t  `probe-large-history.mjs` (long histories, `PROBE_TURNS=N`),\n222\t  `probe-codex-function-call.mjs` (structured tool replay), and\n223\t  `probe-picker.mjs` (pty-driven picker behavior).\n224\t- **Spec verification gaps** *(closed 2026-07-27, claude 2.1.220 /\n225\t  codex-cli 0.145.0)*. Every unknown the two spec docs listed has been\n226\t  probed and each is now marked RESOLVED in place with its date and CLI\n227\t  version. (Minor pre-existing notes elsewhere in those docs — the `version`\n228\t  string's cosmetic role, sidechain turns — were out of scope and are\n229\t  untouched.) Findings that changed behavior:\n230\t  - **Duplicate `uuid` silently drops a turn** — resume still exits 0 with no\n231\t    warning. The one genuinely load-bearing invariant of the four; the other\n232\t    three (out-of-order timestamps, `sessionId`/filename mismatch, dangling\n233\t    `parentUuid`) are all tolerated. `src/test/fabricate-invariants.test.ts`\n234\t    guards it.\n235\t  - **Structured tool replay into Codex** — a `function_call` whose `name` is\n236\t    a tool Codex never registered is accepted, so Claude→Codex no longer\n237\t    flattens tool history to prose. Unpaired results stay prose, because an\n238\t    orphaned output carries no tool name at all.\n239\t  - **Bridged sessions are titled by their conversation**, not by the import\n240\t    notice: the picker reads `ai-title`, which fabricated sessions lacked.\n241\t  - Timestamps are normalized to millisecond UTC on write rather than trusting\n242\t    tolerance nobody measured; the fake `estimateTokens` heuristic is gone in\n243\t    favor of characters/bytes, now reported on the fabrication path too.\n244\t  Deliberately left unmeasured: histories beyond ~300 turns (both targets are\n245\t  clean to there, and fitting more is the target CLI's own job),\n246\t  `custom_tool_call` fabrication, and which key the Claude picker sorts on.\n247\t- **Encrypted-reasoning replay** *(shipped in 0.2.0)*\n248\t  — Codex's `reasoning` items are ciphertext only OpenAI's servers can\n249\t  decrypt; conversation-ledger 0.10.0 preserves them losslessly and opaquely\n250\t  (`kind: \"reasoning\"`, ciphertext in `raw.data`, `content` a bare opacity\n251\t  marker). `listConversations` (`src/conversations.ts`) now fetches\n252\t  `reasoning` events alongside `conversation_turn`s — `readEvents`'s `kind`\n253\t  filter is exact-match only, so it fetches unfiltered and narrows\n254\t  client-side — and carries them through `ConversationSummary.events` in\n255\t  seq order; `turnCount` still counts only visible turns.\n256\t  `src/targets/codex.ts`'s `buildRolloutLines` replays each reasoning\n257\t  event's verbatim `raw.data` response_item line at its correct seq position\n258\t  when `event.producer.source === \"codex\"` — gated per-event, not\n259\t  per-conversation, even though today's data model (a `ConversationSummary`\n260\t  groups by one native session id, hence one source) makes that equivalent\n261\t  to a per-conversation check in practice; genuine multi-hop carry-over\n262\t  (reconstructing an ancestor hop's reasoning via the lineage chain when\n263\t  re-bridging) is deliberately not built and stays a follow-up. Never\n264\t  applies to a Claude Code target: foreign reasoning can't be forged as a\n265\t  native reasoning item, so the only real switch is whether provider-matched\n266\t  replay happens, not what it's coerced into — unreadable/foreign reasoning\n267\t  is still folded into labeled text as before. On by default (decided\n268\t  2026-07-23); opt out with `--no-reasoning-replay` or\n269\t  `{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` in `~/.turnbridge/config.json` (`src/config.ts`).\n270\t  No ownership gate — replay applies to any synced conversation, not just\n271\t  the current user's own, per 2026-07-23 decision. The fabricated session's\n272\t  import notice (`buildRolloutLines`) is conditional: it discloses the\n273\t  replayed-block count when replay happened instead of the blanket \"hidden\n274\t  reasoning ... not transferred\" claim, and still states that claim for\n275\t  everything else in the same session. Empirically verified via\n276\t  scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs (manual injection, independent of\n277\t  this integration): same-account and cross-account replay of a real blob in\n278\t  a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI versions, and a\n279\t  different paid ChatGPT account (identical token accounting), so the\n280\t  ciphertext is not keyed per-account; the free tier cannot run Codex at all.\n281\t  Still open: blob TTL, and whether API-platform-org auth behaves the same\n282\t  as ChatGPT auth — turnbridge attempts replay unconditionally either way\n283\t  since auth mode isn't observable from here; a rejected blob at actual\n284\t  `codex resume` time has no proactive detection and falls under the same\n285\t  `--bootstrap` escape hatch as any other fabrication-drift failure.\n286\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_016ibQ2LDDEZhhok3snU8r1s","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"defab1d48ffb334ef077b543c74110f5a05efd71","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","seq":40},"id":"ev1-32b482d13a3de29c074d4f0cfb29dbd2f8f2941833f071821aa20f7c872badd8","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-12T01:20:41.171Z","producer":{"session_id":"60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.228","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"content":"1\t# WIP technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** MVP implemented for Claude Code ⇄ Codex ⇄ opencode; fabrication\n4\tadapters are version-pinned and must be revalidated per CLI release.\n5\t\n6\t## Main flow\n7\t\n8\t1. Conversation Ledger's hooks (`cledger install all`) capture each CLI's\n9\t   local transcript incrementally into git notes as normalized\n10\t   `conversation_turn` events, stamped with the repo's git identity\n11\t   (`actor.id` = `user.email`) on human turns. opencode has no transcript file\n12\t   to tail — cledger captures it through a `session.idle` plugin that converts\n13\t   `opencode export`, one event per *part*, since its store is mutable\n14\t   (cledger 0.18.0+).\n15\t2. `turnbridge resume [claude|codex|opencode]` lists conversations compatible with the\n16\t   current repository state: events anchored to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n17\t   (`--any-commit` lifts this), authored by you or unattributed\n18\t   (`--all` includes collaborators).\n19\t3. Selecting a conversation whose source matches the target CLI delegates to\n20\t   that CLI's native resume.\n21\t4. Cross-CLI selections default to **native fabrication**: turnbridge writes a\n22\t   target-native session file from the canonical events and launches the\n23\t   target's own resume on it. `--bootstrap` (also the automatic fallback when\n24\t   fabrication is unsupported or fails validation) starts a fresh target\n25\t   session whose first prompt instructs it to read the literal transcript.\n26\t\n27\t## Module map\n28\t\n29\t- `src/conversations.ts` — ledger read + grouping + ownership/compat filter.\n30\t- `src/resume.ts` — picker flow, target choice, default-target config.\n31\t- `src/targets/*` — per-CLI `TargetAdapter`: `nativeResume`, `fabricate`,\n32\t  `bootstrap`. Fabrication throws `FabricationUnsupportedError` to trigger the\n33\t  fallback.\n34\t- `src/transcript.ts` + `src/bootstrap.ts` — literal markdown rendering and\n35\t  the rehydration prompt; reports estimated token size before launch.\n36\t- `src/shim.ts` — opt-in PATH shims: bare `claude --resume` / `codex resume`\n37\t  open the merged picker; everything else passes through to the real binary.\n38\t  No opencode shim: it has no argument-free \"show me my sessions\" invocation to\n39\t  interpose on (`-s` always takes an id), and intercepting one that takes an\n40\t  argument would both discard the id and recurse through the shim.\n41\t\n42\t## Division of labor with Conversation Ledger\n43\t\n44\tThe ledger owns capture (adapters, cursors, idempotent event ids, git-notes\n45\tstorage, transport). Turnbridge never parses source transcripts itself and\n46\tis a reader plus a writer of *target-native* session files. It writes exactly\n47\tone kind of ledger event — `continuation` lineage edges (below) — and no\n48\tconversation content. Ledger changes made for turnbridge: a library entry\n49\tpoint (`package.json` exports) and human-turn identity stamping.\n50\t\n51\tTurnbridge's reads inherit cledger 0.8.0's read-time behavior: `readEvents()`\n52\tlazily absorbs whatever transport already fetched into the local ref, then\n53\tauto-runs squash/rebase re-anchor detection, which can append a `re_anchor`\n54\tmapping event and print a notice to stderr. Reachability filtering resolves\n55\tthrough `re_anchor` mappings, so a bridged conversation and its `continuation`\n56\tlineage survive a squash merge instead of falling out of the picker. Turnbridge\n57\tissues two such reads per invocation (conversation listing, then lineage\n58\tlookup) and buffers the ledger's stderr notices across both so they render as\n59\tone block above the picker rather than interleaving with it.\n60\t\n61\t## Conversation lineage (branch-on-bridge)\n62\t\n63\tBridging is a fork, not a move. Fabricating a target session writes a real\n64\tnative file; when the user continues there, the target CLI captures it as a\n65\tnew conversation whose id is the session id turnbridge generated (verified:\n66\tCodex resume preserves the id and appends). The source conversation still\n67\texists, so both are resumable — a genuine branch, like a git fork.\n68\t\n69\tTo keep that honest instead of confusing, at fabricate time turnbridge appends\n70\ta `continuation` event (`links: [{rel:\"continues\", target:<source>}]`, content\n71\tcarries `imported_through_seq`) belonging to the target conversation. The\n72\tpicker reads these and labels rows in both directions (`→ bridged to Codex`,\n73\t`↳ continued from Claude Code`); re-bridging a conversation that already\n74\tembeds imported history is allowed but flagged. The default listing shows\n75\tboth branch endpoints — abandonment is not assumed. The same edge is what lets\n76\ta downstream consumer (intent-recall) de-duplicate the copied prefix: target\n77\tturns `0..imported_through_seq` are known copies of the source, not fresh\n78\tevidence, so turnbridge does not need to alter capture to avoid double-count.\n79\t\n80\t## Fabrication contract (per target adapter)\n81\t\n82\t**Governing rule: the fabricated session has to work in the target.** Fidelity\n83\tto the source is a property of a session that functions, not a substitute for\n84\tone. Where a target's native records demand a particular shape — a resolvable\n85\tprovider id, a specific part structure — match that shape, and carry the\n86\tsource's identity somewhere that does not break resume. A bridged session that\n87\tis unimpeachably honest and cannot be continued has failed at the only thing it\n88\texists to do. *(Corrected 2026-08-06, after `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` silently\n89\tbricked continuation on every bridged opencode session — see the opencode notes\n90\tbelow.)*\n91\t\n92\tEvery field a fabricated session carries falls into one of three tiers, and the\n93\ttier decides where the logic lives:\n94\t\n95\t1. **Portable** — the same meaning in every target: turn role, visible text,\n96\t   ordering, timestamps. Shared code writes these once; adapters do not touch\n97\t   them.\n98\t2. **Portable by default, overridden where a target requires it** — usually\n99\t   transferable, but some targets reject or mishandle the generic form, so the\n100\t   adapter substitutes a working value and discloses the substitution. The\n101\t   source model id is the type case; foreign tool calls are the other\n102\t   (structured where the target accepts unknown names, labeled text where not).\n103\t3. **Target-specific** — no portable form exists and the adapter owns it\n104\t   outright: opencode's `providerID`/`projectID` and its `step-start`/\n105\t   `step-finish` part scaffolding, Claude's `parentUuid` chain, Codex's paired\n106\t   `event_msg`/`response_item` twins.\n107\t\n108\tTiers 2 and 3 are what the adapter abstraction exists for. Discovering that a\n109\tfield assumed to be tier 1 is really tier 2 is a contract change worth\n110\trecording here, not a quiet workaround inside one adapter.\n111\t\n112\t- Pin the CLI versions the writer was validated against; on an unknown version\n113\t  warn and offer bootstrap.\n114\t- Write only new session files under the CLI's own session directory; never\n115\t  modify existing native sessions.\n116\t- Represent foreign tool calls faithfully (or as clearly-labeled text when the\n117\t  target rejects unknown tool schemas — see per-adapter spec notes).\n118\t- Propagate the source model id into fabricated assistant envelopes verbatim\n119\t  **where the target can resolve it** (decided 2026-07-21, amended 2026-08-06).\n120\t  If the target recognizes it — e.g. the user registered matching external\n121\t  models in Claude Code — the session restores seamlessly. If it does not, write\n122\t  a value the target *can* resolve and disclose the real source model in the\n123\t  import notice; do not leave an unresolvable id in a field the target uses to\n124\t  dispatch. The original rule (\"never substitute a recognized-but-false id\")\n125\t  was written to keep the transcript honest and instead produced sessions that\n126\t  could not be continued at all; honesty belongs in the disclosure, not in a\n127\t  dispatch field.\n128\t\n129\tReverse-engineered, empirically verified format specs (pinned versions,\n130\tminimal working schemas, unknowns): [docs/specs/claude-session-format.md](specs/claude-session-format.md),\n131\t[docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md](specs/codex-rollout-format.md), and\n132\t[docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md](specs/opencode-session-format.md).\n133\tKey constraints: Claude resume is scoped to the project dir encoded from the\n134\tlaunch cwd and reconstructs history by walking `parentUuid` from the last\n135\tline; Codex resume-by-id scans `~/.codex/sessions` for a matching filename +\n136\t`session_meta` id, and only plain `message` response_items are verified-safe\n137\tto replay (foreign tool calls fold into labeled text). Claude accepts foreign\n138\t`tool_use` names verbatim, so Codex→Claude keeps structured tool history.\n139\t\n140\topencode is the first target that breaks the \"write a new session file\"\n141\tassumption: every session lives in one shared SQLite DB, so fabrication builds\n142\ta payload in opencode's own export format and shells out to `opencode import`.\n143\tIt is also the target that taught the tier model its lesson twice over: the\n144\tsession's stored `providerID` is what its agent loop dispatches from (an\n145\tunresolvable id is fatal, not cosmetic), and **ids are an ordering key** —\n146\tparts render in `id` order, and message ids must sort ahead of the ones\n147\topencode mints next, or a resumed session answers and then keeps generating.\n148\tBoth are tier 3, and both looked fine on screen while being broken underneath.\n149\tThree further consequences the file-based targets don't have. Import is **not atomic**\n150\t— a payload rejected part-way leaves a truncated session behind, so a failed\n151\timport deletes it before falling back to bootstrap. Message order comes from\n152\t`time.created` rather than payload position, so the import notice is backdated\n153\tahead of history instead of stamped `now` (stamped `now`, it sorted last and\n154\trendered as a `QUEUED` prompt). And `info.projectID` — an opaque id looked up\n155\tvia `opencode debug scrap`, not derivable from the path — is what scopes a\n156\tsession to a repo; the `global` fallback lists it from every directory.\n157\t\n158\t## Sharing model\n159\t\n160\t`actor.id`/`actor.display` come from `git config user.email/name` at capture\n161\ttime. Default listings: own + unattributed conversations; `--all` shows every\n162\tauthor with labels. Transport is optional-but-default-on: Conversation\n163\tLedger's auto-installed pre-push hook and fetch refspec ride normal\n164\t`git push`/`git fetch`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\n165\tuser (`{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}`); `cledger sync`\n166\tremains available for an explicit push/fetch/merge. Turnbridge adds no\n167\tnetwork behavior of its own — it only reads whatever the ledger has already\n168\tabsorbed locally. Events captured before identity stamping existed are\n169\tunattributed; a forced transcript rescan after the upgrade can duplicate\n170\tthose turns under new event ids.\n171\t\n172\t## Risks\n173\t\n174\tNative formats change without notice (fabrication is version-pinned, bootstrap\n175\tis the recovery path); transcript content can include secrets (the ledger\n176\tredacts at capture and sync time, but matching is pattern/entropy-based and\n177\tcannot catch every secret — review before sharing still matters); full\n178\thistories can exceed the target's context (size is reported before launch);\n179\tcrashes mid-turn leave partial capture, which the ledger tolerates by design.\n180\t\n181\t## Roadmap\n182\t\n183\tToward an npm release (decided 2026-07-21; deepen the two existing targets\n184\tbefore broadening):\n185\t\n186\t- Publish `conversation-ledger` to npm (name is unclaimed), then swap the\n187\t  `file:` dependency for a semver range — `prepublishOnly` refuses to publish\n188\t  until this happens. Deliberately deferred while both packages are iterated\n189\t  on and dogfooded for a few days.\n190\t- Adapter-drift automation stays **reporting-only** (decided 2026-08-02). The\n191\t  daily check (`.github/workflows/adapter-drift.yml`) opens a tracking issue on\n192\t  drift and now closes it when drift clears; it never writes to the repo.\n193\t  Auto-drafting the revalidation PR was built and then reverted. The reasoning\n194\t  is worth keeping, because the idea is tempting enough to recur: CI cannot\n195\t  validate a pin — the probes need both target CLIs installed, a TTY for the\n196\t  interactive smoke test, and paid model credentials per provider to make the\n197\t  recall turns real. So any PR CI opened would be a mechanical one-line bump\n198\t  with nothing behind it, saving a trivial edit while creating a *mergeable*\n199\t  artifact that asserts a validation nobody performed. An issue cannot be\n200\t  merged, and that asymmetry is the point. The agent-drafted variant (the old\n201\t  stage 2, needing an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret) fails for the same reason —\n202\t  the API key was never the binding constraint; the provider credentials the\n203\t  probes need are, and no amount of agency in CI conjures them.\n204\t- **opencode is a full member** *(target added 2026-08-02, opencode 1.18.5;\n205\t  capture landed in conversation-ledger 0.18.0 and verified here 2026-08-03)*.\n206\t  Both directions are exercised: a Claude Code conversation bridged into\n207\t  opencode is captured back by cledger's `session.idle` plugin, and bridging\n208\t  that opencode conversation onward into Codex and Claude Code preserves the\n209\t  tool call as a structured `function_call`/`function_call_output` pair and as\n210\t  `tool_use`/`tool_result` respectively, with the source model id intact.\n211\t  Note the asymmetry cledger's README calls out: opencode `reasoning` parts are\n212\t  plaintext, so they arrive as ordinary visible `thinking` blocks rather than\n213\t  as Codex-style opaque ciphertext — nothing here replays provider-private\n214\t  state for this target. Remaining opencode gaps: no headless model-recall\n215\t  probe (the Codex analogue is `probe-codex-content.mjs`), and long histories\n216\t  unmeasured — see the spec's \"Unknowns\".\n217\t- Revalidation tooling exists and is the bar for widening version pins:\n218\t  `npm run smoke:interactive` (real-TUI render check, all three targets),\n219\t  `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs` (headless model-recall probe),\n220\t  `probe-session-invariants.mjs` (malformed-session tolerance),\n221\t  `probe-large-history.mjs` (long histories, `PROBE_TURNS=N`),\n222\t  `probe-codex-function-call.mjs` (structured tool replay), and\n223\t  `probe-picker.mjs` (pty-driven picker behavior).\n224\t- **Spec verification gaps** *(closed 2026-07-27, claude 2.1.220 /\n225\t  codex-cli 0.145.0)*. Every unknown the two spec docs listed has been\n226\t  probed and each is now marked RESOLVED in place with its date and CLI\n227\t  version. (Minor pre-existing notes elsewhere in those docs — the `version`\n228\t  string's cosmetic role, sidechain turns — were out of scope and are\n229\t  untouched.) Findings that changed behavior:\n230\t  - **Duplicate `uuid` silently drops a turn** — resume still exits 0 with no\n231\t    warning. The one genuinely load-bearing invariant of the four; the other\n232\t    three (out-of-order timestamps, `sessionId`/filename mismatch, dangling\n233\t    `parentUuid`) are all tolerated. `src/test/fabricate-invariants.test.ts`\n234\t    guards it.\n235\t  - **Structured tool replay into Codex** — a `function_call` whose `name` is\n236\t    a tool Codex never registered is accepted, so Claude→Codex no longer\n237\t    flattens tool history to prose. Unpaired results stay prose, because an\n238\t    orphaned output carries no tool name at all.\n239\t  - **Bridged sessions are titled by their conversation**, not by the import\n240\t    notice: the picker reads `ai-title`, which fabricated sessions lacked.\n241\t  - Timestamps are normalized to millisecond UTC on write rather than trusting\n242\t    tolerance nobody measured; the fake `estimateTokens` heuristic is gone in\n243\t    favor of characters/bytes, now reported on the fabrication path too.\n244\t  Deliberately left unmeasured: histories beyond ~300 turns (both targets are\n245\t  clean to there, and fitting more is the target CLI's own job),\n246\t  `custom_tool_call` fabrication, and which key the Claude picker sorts on.\n247\t- **Encrypted-reasoning replay** *(shipped in 0.2.0)*\n248\t  — Codex's `reasoning` items are ciphertext only OpenAI's servers can\n249\t  decrypt; conversation-ledger 0.10.0 preserves them losslessly and opaquely\n250\t  (`kind: \"reasoning\"`, ciphertext in `raw.data`, `content` a bare opacity\n251\t  marker). `listConversations` (`src/conversations.ts`) now fetches\n252\t  `reasoning` events alongside `conversation_turn`s — `readEvents`'s `kind`\n253\t  filter is exact-match only, so it fetches unfiltered and narrows\n254\t  client-side — and carries them through `ConversationSummary.events` in\n255\t  seq order; `turnCount` still counts only visible turns.\n256\t  `src/targets/codex.ts`'s `buildRolloutLines` replays each reasoning\n257\t  event's verbatim `raw.data` response_item line at its correct seq position\n258\t  when `event.producer.source === \"codex\"` — gated per-event, not\n259\t  per-conversation, even though today's data model (a `ConversationSummary`\n260\t  groups by one native session id, hence one source) makes that equivalent\n261\t  to a per-conversation check in practice; genuine multi-hop carry-over\n262\t  (reconstructing an ancestor hop's reasoning via the lineage chain when\n263\t  re-bridging) is deliberately not built and stays a follow-up. Never\n264\t  applies to a Claude Code target: foreign reasoning can't be forged as a\n265\t  native reasoning item, so the only real switch is whether provider-matched\n266\t  replay happens, not what it's coerced into — unreadable/foreign reasoning\n267\t  is still folded into labeled text as before. On by default (decided\n268\t  2026-07-23); opt out with `--no-reasoning-replay` or\n269\t  `{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` in `~/.turnbridge/config.json` (`src/config.ts`).\n270\t  No ownership gate — replay applies to any synced conversation, not just\n271\t  the current user's own, per 2026-07-23 decision. The fabricated session's\n272\t  import notice (`buildRolloutLines`) is conditional: it discloses the\n273\t  replayed-block count when replay happened instead of the blanket \"hidden\n274\t  reasoning ... not transferred\" claim, and still states that claim for\n275\t  everything else in the same session. Empirically verified via\n276\t  scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs (manual injection, independent of\n277\t  this integration): same-account and cross-account replay of a real blob in\n278\t  a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI versions, and a\n279\t  different paid ChatGPT account (identical token accounting), so the\n280\t  ciphertext is not keyed per-account; the free tier cannot run Codex at all.\n281\t  Still open: blob TTL, and whether API-platform-org auth behaves the same\n282\t  as ChatGPT auth — turnbridge attempts replay unconditionally either way\n283\t  since auth mode isn't observable from here; a rejected blob at actual\n284\t  `codex resume` time has no proactive detection and falls under the same\n285\t  `--bootstrap` escape hatch as any other fabrication-drift failure.\n286\t","tool_use_id":"toolu_016ibQ2LDDEZhhok3snU8r1s","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"parentUuid":"ce703a07-84a8-46bc-a0c3-5c777ab1aa29","promptId":"2dd3d0ba-7359-407e-973c-b425dcc89321","sessionId":"60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","sessionKind":"bg","session_id":"60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"ce703a07-84a8-46bc-a0c3-5c777ab1aa29","timestamp":"2026-08-12T01:20:41.171Z","toolUseResult":{"file":{"content":"# WIP technical design\n\n**Status:** MVP implemented for Claude Code ⇄ Codex ⇄ opencode; fabrication\nadapters are version-pinned and must be revalidated per CLI release.\n\n## Main flow\n\n1. Conversation Ledger's hooks (`cledger install all`) capture each CLI's\n   local transcript incrementally into git notes as normalized\n   `conversation_turn` events, stamped with the repo's git identity\n   (`actor.id` = `user.email`) on human turns. opencode has no transcript file\n   to tail — cledger captures it through a `session.idle` plugin that converts\n   `opencode export`, one event per *part*, since its store is mutable\n   (cledger 0.18.0+).\n2. `turnbridge resume [claude|codex|opencode]` lists conversations compatible with the\n   current repository state: events anchored to commits reachable from `HEAD`\n   (`--any-commit` lifts this), authored by you or unattributed\n   (`--all` includes collaborators).\n3. Selecting a conversation whose source matches the target CLI delegates to\n   that CLI's native resume.\n4. Cross-CLI selections default to **native fabrication**: turnbridge writes a\n   target-native session file from the canonical events and launches the\n   target's own resume on it. `--bootstrap` (also the automatic fallback when\n   fabrication is unsupported or fails validation) starts a fresh target\n   session whose first prompt instructs it to read the literal transcript.\n\n## Module map\n\n- `src/conversations.ts` — ledger read + grouping + ownership/compat filter.\n- `src/resume.ts` — picker flow, target choice, default-target config.\n- `src/targets/*` — per-CLI `TargetAdapter`: `nativeResume`, `fabricate`,\n  `bootstrap`. Fabrication throws `FabricationUnsupportedError` to trigger the\n  fallback.\n- `src/transcript.ts` + `src/bootstrap.ts` — literal markdown rendering and\n  the rehydration prompt; reports estimated token size before launch.\n- `src/shim.ts` — opt-in PATH shims: bare `claude --resume` / `codex resume`\n  open the merged picker; everything else passes through to the real binary.\n  No opencode shim: it has no argument-free \"show me my sessions\" invocation to\n  interpose on (`-s` always takes an id), and intercepting one that takes an\n  argument would both discard the id and recurse through the shim.\n\n## Division of labor with Conversation Ledger\n\nThe ledger owns capture (adapters, cursors, idempotent event ids, git-notes\nstorage, transport). Turnbridge never parses source transcripts itself and\nis a reader plus a writer of *target-native* session files. It writes exactly\none kind of ledger event — `continuation` lineage edges (below) — and no\nconversation content. Ledger changes made for turnbridge: a library entry\npoint (`package.json` exports) and human-turn identity stamping.\n\nTurnbridge's reads inherit cledger 0.8.0's read-time behavior: `readEvents()`\nlazily absorbs whatever transport already fetched into the local ref, then\nauto-runs squash/rebase re-anchor detection, which can append a `re_anchor`\nmapping event and print a notice to stderr. Reachability filtering resolves\nthrough `re_anchor` mappings, so a bridged conversation and its `continuation`\nlineage survive a squash merge instead of falling out of the picker. Turnbridge\nissues two such reads per invocation (conversation listing, then lineage\nlookup) and buffers the ledger's stderr notices across both so they render as\none block above the picker rather than interleaving with it.\n\n## Conversation lineage (branch-on-bridge)\n\nBridging is a fork, not a move. Fabricating a target session writes a real\nnative file; when the user continues there, the target CLI captures it as a\nnew conversation whose id is the session id turnbridge generated (verified:\nCodex resume preserves the id and appends). The source conversation still\nexists, so both are resumable — a genuine branch, like a git fork.\n\nTo keep that honest instead of confusing, at fabricate time turnbridge appends\na `continuation` event (`links: [{rel:\"continues\", target:<source>}]`, content\ncarries `imported_through_seq`) belonging to the target conversation. The\npicker reads these and labels rows in both directions (`→ bridged to Codex`,\n`↳ continued from Claude Code`); re-bridging a conversation that already\nembeds imported history is allowed but flagged. The default listing shows\nboth branch endpoints — abandonment is not assumed. The same edge is what lets\na downstream consumer (intent-recall) de-duplicate the copied prefix: target\nturns `0..imported_through_seq` are known copies of the source, not fresh\nevidence, so turnbridge does not need to alter capture to avoid double-count.\n\n## Fabrication contract (per target adapter)\n\n**Governing rule: the fabricated session has to work in the target.** Fidelity\nto the source is a property of a session that functions, not a substitute for\none. Where a target's native records demand a particular shape — a resolvable\nprovider id, a specific part structure — match that shape, and carry the\nsource's identity somewhere that does not break resume. A bridged session that\nis unimpeachably honest and cannot be continued has failed at the only thing it\nexists to do. *(Corrected 2026-08-06, after `providerID: \"turnbridge\"` silently\nbricked continuation on every bridged opencode session — see the opencode notes\nbelow.)*\n\nEvery field a fabricated session carries falls into one of three tiers, and the\ntier decides where the logic lives:\n\n1. **Portable** — the same meaning in every target: turn role, visible text,\n   ordering, timestamps. Shared code writes these once; adapters do not touch\n   them.\n2. **Portable by default, overridden where a target requires it** — usually\n   transferable, but some targets reject or mishandle the generic form, so the\n   adapter substitutes a working value and discloses the substitution. The\n   source model id is the type case; foreign tool calls are the other\n   (structured where the target accepts unknown names, labeled text where not).\n3. **Target-specific** — no portable form exists and the adapter owns it\n   outright: opencode's `providerID`/`projectID` and its `step-start`/\n   `step-finish` part scaffolding, Claude's `parentUuid` chain, Codex's paired\n   `event_msg`/`response_item` twins.\n\nTiers 2 and 3 are what the adapter abstraction exists for. Discovering that a\nfield assumed to be tier 1 is really tier 2 is a contract change worth\nrecording here, not a quiet workaround inside one adapter.\n\n- Pin the CLI versions the writer was validated against; on an unknown version\n  warn and offer bootstrap.\n- Write only new session files under the CLI's own session directory; never\n  modify existing native sessions.\n- Represent foreign tool calls faithfully (or as clearly-labeled text when the\n  target rejects unknown tool schemas — see per-adapter spec notes).\n- Propagate the source model id into fabricated assistant envelopes verbatim\n  **where the target can resolve it** (decided 2026-07-21, amended 2026-08-06).\n  If the target recognizes it — e.g. the user registered matching external\n  models in Claude Code — the session restores seamlessly. If it does not, write\n  a value the target *can* resolve and disclose the real source model in the\n  import notice; do not leave an unresolvable id in a field the target uses to\n  dispatch. The original rule (\"never substitute a recognized-but-false id\")\n  was written to keep the transcript honest and instead produced sessions that\n  could not be continued at all; honesty belongs in the disclosure, not in a\n  dispatch field.\n\nReverse-engineered, empirically verified format specs (pinned versions,\nminimal working schemas, unknowns): [docs/specs/claude-session-format.md](specs/claude-session-format.md),\n[docs/specs/codex-rollout-format.md](specs/codex-rollout-format.md), and\n[docs/specs/opencode-session-format.md](specs/opencode-session-format.md).\nKey constraints: Claude resume is scoped to the project dir encoded from the\nlaunch cwd and reconstructs history by walking `parentUuid` from the last\nline; Codex resume-by-id scans `~/.codex/sessions` for a matching filename +\n`session_meta` id, and only plain `message` response_items are verified-safe\nto replay (foreign tool calls fold into labeled text). Claude accepts foreign\n`tool_use` names verbatim, so Codex→Claude keeps structured tool history.\n\nopencode is the first target that breaks the \"write a new session file\"\nassumption: every session lives in one shared SQLite DB, so fabrication builds\na payload in opencode's own export format and shells out to `opencode import`.\nIt is also the target that taught the tier model its lesson twice over: the\nsession's stored `providerID` is what its agent loop dispatches from (an\nunresolvable id is fatal, not cosmetic), and **ids are an ordering key** —\nparts render in `id` order, and message ids must sort ahead of the ones\nopencode mints next, or a resumed session answers and then keeps generating.\nBoth are tier 3, and both looked fine on screen while being broken underneath.\nThree further consequences the file-based targets don't have. Import is **not atomic**\n— a payload rejected part-way leaves a truncated session behind, so a failed\nimport deletes it before falling back to bootstrap. Message order comes from\n`time.created` rather than payload position, so the import notice is backdated\nahead of history instead of stamped `now` (stamped `now`, it sorted last and\nrendered as a `QUEUED` prompt). And `info.projectID` — an opaque id looked up\nvia `opencode debug scrap`, not derivable from the path — is what scopes a\nsession to a repo; the `global` fallback lists it from every directory.\n\n## Sharing model\n\n`actor.id`/`actor.display` come from `git config user.email/name` at capture\ntime. Default listings: own + unattributed conversations; `--all` shows every\nauthor with labels. Transport is optional-but-default-on: Conversation\nLedger's auto-installed pre-push hook and fetch refspec ride normal\n`git push`/`git fetch`, gated by the ledger's secret scan and disableable per\nuser (`{\"transport\": {\"hook\": false, \"fetchRefspec\": false}}`); `cledger sync`\nremains available for an explicit push/fetch/merge. Turnbridge adds no\nnetwork behavior of its own — it only reads whatever the ledger has already\nabsorbed locally. Events captured before identity stamping existed are\nunattributed; a forced transcript rescan after the upgrade can duplicate\nthose turns under new event ids.\n\n## Risks\n\nNative formats change without notice (fabrication is version-pinned, bootstrap\nis the recovery path); transcript content can include secrets (the ledger\nredacts at capture and sync time, but matching is pattern/entropy-based and\ncannot catch every secret — review before sharing still matters); full\nhistories can exceed the target's context (size is reported before launch);\ncrashes mid-turn leave partial capture, which the ledger tolerates by design.\n\n## Roadmap\n\nToward an npm release (decided 2026-07-21; deepen the two existing targets\nbefore broadening):\n\n- Publish `conversation-ledger` to npm (name is unclaimed), then swap the\n  `file:` dependency for a semver range — `prepublishOnly` refuses to publish\n  until this happens. Deliberately deferred while both packages are iterated\n  on and dogfooded for a few days.\n- Adapter-drift automation stays **reporting-only** (decided 2026-08-02). The\n  daily check (`.github/workflows/adapter-drift.yml`) opens a tracking issue on\n  drift and now closes it when drift clears; it never writes to the repo.\n  Auto-drafting the revalidation PR was built and then reverted. The reasoning\n  is worth keeping, because the idea is tempting enough to recur: CI cannot\n  validate a pin — the probes need both target CLIs installed, a TTY for the\n  interactive smoke test, and paid model credentials per provider to make the\n  recall turns real. So any PR CI opened would be a mechanical one-line bump\n  with nothing behind it, saving a trivial edit while creating a *mergeable*\n  artifact that asserts a validation nobody performed. An issue cannot be\n  merged, and that asymmetry is the point. The agent-drafted variant (the old\n  stage 2, needing an `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` secret) fails for the same reason —\n  the API key was never the binding constraint; the provider credentials the\n  probes need are, and no amount of agency in CI conjures them.\n- **opencode is a full member** *(target added 2026-08-02, opencode 1.18.5;\n  capture landed in conversation-ledger 0.18.0 and verified here 2026-08-03)*.\n  Both directions are exercised: a Claude Code conversation bridged into\n  opencode is captured back by cledger's `session.idle` plugin, and bridging\n  that opencode conversation onward into Codex and Claude Code preserves the\n  tool call as a structured `function_call`/`function_call_output` pair and as\n  `tool_use`/`tool_result` respectively, with the source model id intact.\n  Note the asymmetry cledger's README calls out: opencode `reasoning` parts are\n  plaintext, so they arrive as ordinary visible `thinking` blocks rather than\n  as Codex-style opaque ciphertext — nothing here replays provider-private\n  state for this target. Remaining opencode gaps: no headless model-recall\n  probe (the Codex analogue is `probe-codex-content.mjs`), and long histories\n  unmeasured — see the spec's \"Unknowns\".\n- Revalidation tooling exists and is the bar for widening version pins:\n  `npm run smoke:interactive` (real-TUI render check, all three targets),\n  `scripts/probe-codex-content.mjs` (headless model-recall probe),\n  `probe-session-invariants.mjs` (malformed-session tolerance),\n  `probe-large-history.mjs` (long histories, `PROBE_TURNS=N`),\n  `probe-codex-function-call.mjs` (structured tool replay), and\n  `probe-picker.mjs` (pty-driven picker behavior).\n- **Spec verification gaps** *(closed 2026-07-27, claude 2.1.220 /\n  codex-cli 0.145.0)*. Every unknown the two spec docs listed has been\n  probed and each is now marked RESOLVED in place with its date and CLI\n  version. (Minor pre-existing notes elsewhere in those docs — the `version`\n  string's cosmetic role, sidechain turns — were out of scope and are\n  untouched.) Findings that changed behavior:\n  - **Duplicate `uuid` silently drops a turn** — resume still exits 0 with no\n    warning. The one genuinely load-bearing invariant of the four; the other\n    three (out-of-order timestamps, `sessionId`/filename mismatch, dangling\n    `parentUuid`) are all tolerated. `src/test/fabricate-invariants.test.ts`\n    guards it.\n  - **Structured tool replay into Codex** — a `function_call` whose `name` is\n    a tool Codex never registered is accepted, so Claude→Codex no longer\n    flattens tool history to prose. Unpaired results stay prose, because an\n    orphaned output carries no tool name at all.\n  - **Bridged sessions are titled by their conversation**, not by the import\n    notice: the picker reads `ai-title`, which fabricated sessions lacked.\n  - Timestamps are normalized to millisecond UTC on write rather than trusting\n    tolerance nobody measured; the fake `estimateTokens` heuristic is gone in\n    favor of characters/bytes, now reported on the fabrication path too.\n  Deliberately left unmeasured: histories beyond ~300 turns (both targets are\n  clean to there, and fitting more is the target CLI's own job),\n  `custom_tool_call` fabrication, and which key the Claude picker sorts on.\n- **Encrypted-reasoning replay** *(shipped in 0.2.0)*\n  — Codex's `reasoning` items are ciphertext only OpenAI's servers can\n  decrypt; conversation-ledger 0.10.0 preserves them losslessly and opaquely\n  (`kind: \"reasoning\"`, ciphertext in `raw.data`, `content` a bare opacity\n  marker). `listConversations` (`src/conversations.ts`) now fetches\n  `reasoning` events alongside `conversation_turn`s — `readEvents`'s `kind`\n  filter is exact-match only, so it fetches unfiltered and narrows\n  client-side — and carries them through `ConversationSummary.events` in\n  seq order; `turnCount` still counts only visible turns.\n  `src/targets/codex.ts`'s `buildRolloutLines` replays each reasoning\n  event's verbatim `raw.data` response_item line at its correct seq position\n  when `event.producer.source === \"codex\"` — gated per-event, not\n  per-conversation, even though today's data model (a `ConversationSummary`\n  groups by one native session id, hence one source) makes that equivalent\n  to a per-conversation check in practice; genuine multi-hop carry-over\n  (reconstructing an ancestor hop's reasoning via the lineage chain when\n  re-bridging) is deliberately not built and stays a follow-up. Never\n  applies to a Claude Code target: foreign reasoning can't be forged as a\n  native reasoning item, so the only real switch is whether provider-matched\n  replay happens, not what it's coerced into — unreadable/foreign reasoning\n  is still folded into labeled text as before. On by default (decided\n  2026-07-23); opt out with `--no-reasoning-replay` or\n  `{\"reasoningReplay\": false}` in `~/.turnbridge/config.json` (`src/config.ts`).\n  No ownership gate — replay applies to any synced conversation, not just\n  the current user's own, per 2026-07-23 decision. The fabricated session's\n  import notice (`buildRolloutLines`) is conditional: it discloses the\n  replayed-block count when replay happened instead of the blanket \"hidden\n  reasoning ... not transferred\" claim, and still states that claim for\n  everything else in the same session. Empirically verified via\n  scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs (manual injection, independent of\n  this integration): same-account and cross-account replay of a real blob in\n  a fabricated session is accepted across sessions, CLI versions, and a\n  different paid ChatGPT account (identical token accounting), so the\n  ciphertext is not keyed per-account; the free tier cannot run Codex at all.\n  Still open: blob TTL, and whether API-platform-org auth behaves the same\n  as ChatGPT auth — turnbridge attempts replay unconditionally either way\n  since auth mode isn't observable from here; a rejected blob at actual\n  `codex resume` time has no proactive detection and falls under the same\n  `--bootstrap` escape hatch as any other fabrication-drift failure.\n","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge/docs/WIP_TECHNICAL_DESIGN.md","numLines":286,"startLine":1,"totalLines":286},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"f2caa035-a292-477c-b898-46598d95721d","version":"2.1.228"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-12T01:24:34.427Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t# WIP technical design\n2\t\n3\t**Status:** exploratory impressions, nothing locked in — including the\n4\timplementation language. This records how the problem looks after building\n5\tconversation-ledger and turnbridge, so later work does not start cold.\n6\t\n7\t## Node model (draft)\n8\t\n9\tNodes are **binned by type** — `conversation`, `file`, `commit`, and whatever\n10\tlater proves necessary — rather than forced into one flat shared namespace.\n11\tAn identifier is therefore a `(type, id)` pair, with the id scheme chosen\n12\tper type. No node taxonomy is committed; the test for adding a type is\n13\twhether it serves a real consumer, not completeness of some ontology.\n14\t\n15\tA node references its underlying artifact; it does not copy content. Content\n16\talready lives in the working tree, git objects, or ledger notes, and the\n17\tgraph points there.\n18\t\n19\tWithin conversations, the current leaning is that the atomic node is the\n20\t**individual ledger event** (a turn), with the conversation as a grouping\n21\tover its events rather than an opaque atom. The ledger records `context` per\n22\tevent, so this granularity falls out of the data naturally — a conversation\n23\tthat spans several commits anchors event-by-event instead of pretending to\n24\tone code state.\n25\t\n26\t**File identity is versioned:** a file node denotes\n27\t`path @ commit (+ uncommitted-worktree state)`, not a bare path — a\n28\tconversation that touched `src/auth.ts` in March relates to that file *as it\n29\twas*, not as it is today. Renames should be tracked so a file's lineage\n30\tsurvives `git mv`. Two known complications, recorded rather than solved:\n31\t\n32\t- The ledger currently captures a *fingerprint* of uncommitted state (sha256\n33\t  of `git status --porcelain`), not its content, so a node keyed on\n34\t  uncommitted state can identify that state but generally cannot reconstruct\n35\t  it — it may reference content that no longer exists anywhere.\n36\t  conversation-ledger is expected to record more granular worktree state,\n37\t  which may close this gap; this design should track that work rather than\n38\t  solve it here.\n39\t- Rename detection (git's `--follow` machinery) is heuristic, which is in\n40\t  tension with deterministic rebuilds; the derivation must pin whatever\n41\t  detection parameters it uses.\n42\t\n43\t## Edge model (draft)\n44\t\n45\tEdges are typed and evidence-bearing: `{src, dst, kind, basis}` where `basis`\n46\tpoints at the events / lines / commits that justify the edge. There is **no\n47\tconfidence field**: every edge is mechanically derived from explicit\n48\tevidence, so kind + basis carries all the trust information there is.\n49\tAnything requiring semantic judgment (a conversation that \"discusses the auth\n50\tmodule\" without ever naming a path, embedding similarity, model-inferred\n51\tlinks) is out of scope here — that class of derivation belongs downstream,\n52\tplausibly in intent-recall.\n53\t\n54\tCandidate kinds, all mechanical:\n55\t\n56\t- `continuation` — conversation→conversation, from turnbridge's ledger\n57\t  `links` (`rel:\"continues\"`). Bridged conversations carry\n58\t  `imported_through_seq`; per turnbridge's design, target turns up through it\n59\t  are known copies of the source, so consumers dedup across this edge (the\n60\t  copied prefix is reachable through the pointer, not re-materialized).\n61\t- `anchored_to` — event→commit, from ledger `context.head`: the code state a\n62\t  turn actually ran against (per event, since context is recorded per event).\n63\t- `modifies` — commit→file, from git history itself.\n64\t- `imports` — file→file, from import/require statements.\n65\t- `references` — doc→file (and doc→doc), from links in documentation.\n66\t- `touches` — event→file, from file paths appearing literally in tool-call\n67\t  content blocks (`Read`/`Edit`/`Bash`) and message text. Literal paths only;\n68\t  no semantic matching.\n69\t- `re_anchored_to` — commit→commit, surfacing the ledger's `re_anchor`\n70\t  mapping events (squash/rebase supersession) as structure rather than\n71\t  resolving them away invisibly.\n72\t\n73\tExplicitly dropped from earlier drafts: co-occurrence edges (`shared_file`,\n74\t`same_author`, `temporal_adjacency` — projections and priors a consumer can\n75\tcompute, not evidence), conversation-level `supersession` (nothing in the\n76\tecosystem emits one), `decision` nodes (no producer exists; intent-recall is\n77\ttoo early to design against), and per-edge confidence scores.\n78\t\n79\t## Inputs\n80\t\n81\t1. **Conversation Ledger**, via its library API (`readEvents`, `sortEvents`)\n82\t   or `cledger export`, grouped by `conversation.id` — and read for *all*\n83\t   users' records, since the graph is a whole-project history. (Practical\n84\t   consequence: the graph over collaborators' conversations is only as\n85\t   complete as what transport has synced.)\n86\t2. **Git history** — commits, trees, renames.\n87\t3. **The working tree** — imports for code, links for docs, current\n88\t   uncommitted state.\n89\t\n90\t## Construction and storage\n91\t\n92\tThe graph is computed **live** from those inputs. There is no new storage\n93\tsystem to write or maintain; the only thing worth persisting is a cache so\n94\trepeated queries do not re-derive the world, and a cache must be exactly\n95\tthat — discardable, with the same derivation producing the same graph.\n96\t\n97\tDeterminism is a commitment precisely because everything is mechanical: same\n98\tinputs, same graph. This is what makes caching, diffing, and downstream\n99\treproducibility possible.\n100\t\n101\t## Downstream consumers\n102\t\n103\tContext Graph does not commit to an export format — not `graph.jsonl`, not\n104\tanything else. Consumers adapt the structure to their needs:\n105\t\n106\t- **Agent traversal to