{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\timport { canonicalJson, sha256Hex } from \"./canonical.js\";\n2\timport type { RedactionRecord } from \"./redact/apply.js\";\n3\t\n4\texport const SCHEMA_VERSION = \"conversation-ledger/v1\";\n5\t\n6\t/**\n7\t * The well-known event kinds. `kind` is an open string so downstream tools\n8\t * can extend the ledger without a schema release; unknown kinds are stored\n9\t * verbatim. The ledger never interprets content, whatever the kind.\n10\t *\n11\t * `unrecognized` is the one kind the ledger emits for content it could *not*\n12\t * interpret: an adapter that meets a transcript line type it has no mapping\n13\t * for preserves the line raw-only under `raw.data` rather than dropping it,\n14\t * so a later adapter version can re-normalize (and supersede) it. Its\n15\t * `content` carries only a `{unrecognized_type}` label — the payload lives in\n16\t * `raw` — but identity still separates distinct lines via `conversation.seq`.\n17\t *\n18\t * `reasoning` is for content the ledger deliberately never interprets: a\n19\t * provider-encrypted reasoning/thinking blob (e.g. Codex's `reasoning`\n20\t * response_items) that only the originating provider can decrypt. Its\n21\t * `content` carries only an opacity marker; the ciphertext lives in `raw`,\n22\t * for a consumer to opt into replaying back through the same provider.\n23\t * Unlike `unrecognized`, this is not a placeholder awaiting a smarter\n24\t * cledger version — no future version will ever be able to read it either.\n25\t *\n26\t * The four kinds below `reasoning` cover what a source records *around* the\n27\t * conversation. Every one of them was previously discarded at capture as\n28\t * \"bookkeeping\"; they are kept because the ledger's job is the whole record,\n29\t * and because the things a session declares about itself (which model, which\n30\t * sandbox, which worktree) are exactly what a consumer needs to judge whether\n31\t * a turn still applies. They are non-conversational by construction, so\n32\t * `log`/`show` hide them unless asked — recorded always, displayed on request.\n33\t *\n34\t * `session_state` is a *declaration that holds until restated*: the session's\n35\t * mode, permission mode, model settings, sandbox and approval policy, title,\n36\t * worktree or cwd relocation. Sources restate these freely, and each\n37\t * restatement is its own event — \"still true at this point in the transcript\"\n38\t * is a fact, not a duplicate.\n39\t *\n40\t * `activity` is a *point-in-time occurrence that is not a turn*: a hook run,\n41\t * a turn duration, a token count, a task starting or finishing, a queue\n42\t * operation, a context compaction, an aborted turn, a step boundary.\n43\t *\n44\t * `context_injection` is material the harness inserted into the model's\n45\t * context that no participant typed — Claude Code's `attachment` lines\n46\t * (task reminders, skill listings, diagnostics, pasted files). It is content\n47\t * the model read, which is why it is not `activity`, but nobody said it,\n48\t * which is why it is not `conversation_turn`.\n49\t *\n50\t * `file_snapshot` is intermediate file state: the versions a file passed\n51\t * through between commits. Tool calls cannot reconstruct this — a `Bash`\n52\t * mutation records the command, not the result — so without it the record of\n53\t * how a file got from one commit to the next has holes. Sources describe\n54\t * these versions with pointers into a machine-local cache, so the pointers\n55\t * live in `content` and what the ledger could resolve of them at capture time\n56\t * lives in `resolved`.\n57\t */\n58\texport const KNOWN_KINDS = [\n59\t  \"conversation_turn\",\n60\t  \"decision\",\n61\t  \"document\",\n62\t  \"annotation\",\n63\t  \"redaction\",\n64\t  \"supersession\",\n65\t  \"re_anchor\",\n66\t  \"unrecognized\",\n67\t  \"reasoning\",\n68\t  \"session_state\",\n69\t  \"activity\",\n70\t  \"context_injection\",\n71\t  \"file_snapshot\",\n72\t] as const;\n73\t\n74\t/**\n75\t * The kinds that record machinery around the conversation rather than\n76\t * anything a participant said. `log`/`show` hide these unless `--with-state`\n77\t * (or an explicit `--kind`) asks for them: a session restates its mode and\n78\t * its tracked-file set constantly, and letting that outnumber the turns\n79\t * ten-to-one would make the default view useless. Capture, export, and sync\n80\t * are unaffected — this list is a display default, nothing more.\n81\t */\n82\texport const SESSION_MACHINERY_KINDS = new Set<string>([\n83\t  \"session_state\",\n84\t  \"activity\",\n85\t  \"context_injection\",\n86\t  \"file_snapshot\",\n87\t]);\n88\t\n89\texport interface Actor {\n90\t  /** \"human\" | \"agent\" | \"system\" */\n91\t  type: string;\n92\t  /** Stable identity when known, e.g. git author email or model id. */\n93\t  id?: string;\n94\t  display?: string;\n95\t}\n96\t\n97\texport interface Producer {\n98\t  /** Capture tool that wrote this event, e.g. \"cledger\". */\n99\t  tool: string;\n100\t  /** Capture tool version. Not part of event identity. */\n101\t  version?: string;\n102\t  /** Source system the content came from, e.g. \"claude-code\", \"codex\". */\n103\t  source?: string;\n104\t  /**\n105\t   * The source system's own version — the coding CLI's version, not\n106\t   * cledger's (that is `version`), e.g. \"2.1.220\" for claude-code or\n107\t   * \"0.145.0\" for codex. Not part of event identity.\n108\t   */\n109\t  source_version?: string;\n110\t  /**\n111\t   * Model that served this turn, verbatim as the source names it, e.g.\n112\t   * \"claude-opus-5\" or \"gpt-5.6-sol\". Set only when the source states it\n113\t   * for this turn — never guessed, never carried across turns the source\n114\t   * did not label. Not part of event identity.\n115\t   */\n116\t  model?: string;\n117\t  /**\n118\t   * Inference provider serving `model`, verbatim as