{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Docs updated (§1 \"Where the drop happens\", §3a walking policy, §4 rewritten to your framing). Line citations re-verified against the files.\n\n**Where the actor.id drop happens: nowhere — nothing ever writes it.** None of the three target formats has a per-turn human-author field; they're single-human by construction. All three build user turns from role + content alone (`claude-code.ts:218`, `codex.ts:75`, `opencode.ts:448`). The sharp part is the asymmetry: `transcript.ts:32` renders `## User (${actor.display})`, so **bootstrap preserves attribution that fabrication loses** — the strictly-less-faithful fallback is more faithful than the primary path on this field. That inversion is the real defect. Fix is ordinary tier-2: prefix human turns with the display name when a conversation has >1 human author (or the author isn't you), disclose in the notice. Gated that way it's invisible for solo bridges. The data's already computed and unused — `conversations.ts:62-68` builds `owners`/`ownerDisplays` and no adapter reads either.\n\n**On the walking logic — your three options feel equally arbitrary because they mix two axes.** \"Which CLI am I rendering *to*\" isn't a decision at all: always the current one, under the current tier rules. So your third option isn't an alternative to the other two, it's what fabrication already does, layered on top of whichever you pick. The real axis is *which evidence you render from*, and there neither \"most recent\" nor \"as far back as possible\" works, because **the chain isn't homogeneous**. In claude→codex→opencode→claude, turns 0–20 originated in Claude, 21–35 in Codex, 36–50 in opencode. Every turn has exactly one origin. \"Most recent\" flattens everything to the worst common denominator; \"as far back as possible\" is undefined for turns that didn't exist that far back.\n\nSo it's a per-turn rule, not a per-conversation one: **render each turn from the conversation where that turn originated.** `imported_through_seq` gives you the segment boundaries directly. That's a fourth option you didn't list, and it's the only one that's not arbitrary — the unique policy that never renders from a copy when an original exists.\n\nThree real decisions do survive, and I'd rather name them than pretend it's clean:\n\n1. **As-recorded vs. as-it-would-have-been.** The human at hop 3 *actually saw* the folded text and may have replied to it. Re-sourcing puts content in front of the model that wasn't in the session they experienced. I lean as-it-would-have-been — the fold markers are transport artifacts turnbridge itself introduced, so restoring is undoing our own damage, not inventing history — but the other reading is defensible and the difference is invisible once shipped.\n2. **Ancestor may be unavailable** (transport didn't sync a collaborator's, or it's unreachable from HEAD). Must degrade to the copy and disclose. This is where your `[visible thinking]\\n[visible thinking]` cleanup lives — and the useful consequence is that **unwrapping heuristics become the fallback, not the mechanism.** That matters: regex-unwrapping as a primary strategy is unsafe, since a human can legitimately type `[visible thinking]` and you'd be silently editing their words.\n3. **Ancestral import notices** are real captured user turns. Drop them on re-render, emit one naming the whole chain — safe because it's the one content you can identify with certainty as yours, and necessary because a stale notice names the wrong source CLI for the history under it.\n\n**On cledger: you're right and my earlier suggestion was worse — promoting `continues` upstream would make cledger aware of turnbridge for no gain.** The problem was never that cledger lacks the concept; it's that *consumers hardcode a producer's payload shape*. Two fixes, neither requiring cledger to know what a bridge is: (a) the producer publishes its reader — turnbridge already exports `readLineage`/`ContinuationRecord`, so context-graph depends on that rather than parsing `content` by hand; (b) the generic facility you're describing. Worth noting cledger already has the *input* half — `appendEvents` is public and turnbridge uses it correctly. What's missing is **governance of the namespace**: a convention for third-party kinds/rels (`turnbridge/continuation`) so independent producers can't collide, plus optional payload validation for a registered kind it still doesn't interpret. That's the difference between \"open string, good luck\" and a real extension point.\n\n**Two consequences of your 0.21.0 merge**, since turnbridge is on `file:../conversation-ledger` and already consuming it:\n\n- The redaction-review work touched `src/store.ts` and `src/cli.ts`. Turnbridge's exposure is its two `readEvents` calls and the stderr-notice buffering at `resume.ts:161-167` — worth a smoke run, not obviously broken.\n- Bigger and unrelated to security: **0.21.0 added gemini-cli and qwen-code adapters.** Turnbridge doesn't filter by source (`conversations.ts:72` takes `producer.source` verbatim), so those conversations now appear in the picker labeled by raw string, since `cliLabel` falls through (`types.ts:21`). `buildPlan` only native-resumes when `target.name === summary.source`, which never matches, so they always fabricate — meaning bridging *out* of Gemini/Qwen probably works today as a free feature, but is silently one-way and completely untested.\n\nresult: Answered all four — actor.id is never written by any adapter (no target format has the field; bootstrap is more faithful than fabrication here, which is the real defect), the walking dilemma dissolves once \"render to\" is separated from \"render from\" and resolves to per-turn origin rendering, cledger needs namespace governance rather than a `continues` rel, and the 0.21.0 merge silently made Gemini/Qwen conversations bridgeable-but-untested. Docs updated on `design/policy-split`.","type":"text"}],"