{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"The inventory is smaller and more disciplined than you're expecting — which pushes back on part of your worry, but your objection lands squarely on *my* proposal.\n\n**Every injected marker in the fabrication path, complete — six of them:**\n\n| Marker | Where |\n|---|---|\n| `[visible thinking]` | all 3 adapters |\n| `[tool result]` | all 3 |\n| `[used tool: X]` | codex only |\n| `[historical tool call — output not captured]` | opencode only |\n| `[turnbridge import notice]` | all 3 |\n\nThat's it. No sprawl, no vibe-coded accretion — each one is load-bearing and each has a comment explaining why. **The `## User (${actor.display})` you objected to isn't in that list**: it's in `transcript.ts`, the *bootstrap* path, which writes a markdown file the model is told to `Read`. Markdown headings in a document the model opens are completely in-distribution — that's the one place this is already fine. So the string you flagged is the one that doesn't need fixing.\n\nBut you're right about the thing I actually proposed. Prefixing human turns with `[Alice]` would have been a **seventh** marker, injected into the primary path, to carry a field only the collaborative case needs. That's exactly the creep you're describing, and I'd withdraw it in that form.\n\n**Where I'll push back:** the alternative to a marker often isn't \"less OOD,\" it's \"a false record.\" Strip `[visible thinking]` and paste the text as plain assistant output and the model now believes it *said* that aloud — and may imitate the register, emitting stream-of-consciousness as user-visible prose. A labeled foreign artifact is strange; an unlabeled one is *wrong*, and wrong is the worse distributional failure. You made this argument yourself in your point 3. So the disagreement isn't whether markers earn their place, it's dose and placement.\n\n**The rule I'd propose, three parts:**\n\n1. **A marker is justified only when its absence would make the record false, not merely incomplete.** That keeps all five content markers (each prevents a category error) and kills the attribution prefix (absence is merely incomplete).\n2. **Prefer the target's native affordance over invented prose.** opencode's `reasoning` parts are *plaintext* — so opencode can take Claude's thinking as a native reasoning part with no marker at all. Claude can't (unforgeable signature), Codex can't (ciphertext). So `[visible thinking]` should disappear on the opencode path specifically. That's a real reduction available today.\n3. **Disclose to the human in the terminal, not to the model in the transcript.** `plan.notes` already prints to stdout and never enters context. Model-substitution disclosures, ancestor-unavailable warnings, size reports — the human is the audience for all of it. Only the import notice genuinely needs to be in-context.\n\nOne thing worth probing rather than asserting: Claude's format has `system` and `attachment` line types (`claude-session-format.md:74`), which would be the native home for the import notice instead of a fake user turn. Your two specs disagree on whether they reach the model — `claude-session-format.md:196` calls attachments \"purely informational, not needed for resume,\" while cledger's `context_injection` describes them as \"material the harness inserted into the model's context.\" That's empirical and you have the probe harness for it. If they do reach context, the notice stops being a counterfeit user turn entirely.\n\n**On feasibility — honest answer: no, we don't currently have precise enough pointers.** We have the ancestry (`parentOf`) and the boundary (`imported_through_seq`), so segmenting *is* possible in principle. But resolving a turn to its origin currently requires **positional correspondence**, and fabrication breaks it three ways: it prepends the import notice (shifting everything), skips events whose blocks render empty (`if (blocks.length === 0) continue`), and merges/splits turns (tool results ride on user lines in Claude; Codex emits paired `event_msg`+`response_item` twins). So target position *i* does not map to source seq *i*, and `imported_through_seq` is a seq in the *source* — it tells you how much was copied, not where each piece landed.\n\nThis is exactly why the source-event-id list is worth recording: turnbridge knows the precise ordered mapping at fabricate time and currently throws it away. Cheap to add, no cledger change, and it makes the walk exact. Chains bridged *before* that lands stay imprecise and fall back.\n\n**Fallback: nearest reachable ancestor, not oldest.** Walk up until you find an available original and use the closest one you can reach — each step up strictly removes one round of accumulated mutation, so nearest-available is monotonically better than stopping early and never worse than the local copy. \"Oldest\" isn't a real option, since if you could reach the oldest you wouldn't be falling back at all.\n\n**Your 1/2/3 — agreed, with one narrowing.** You're right that the as-recorded fork is basically confined to fold markers; core content text passes through hops verbatim. Two other things do mutate, both minor: tool results folded to prose, and assistant turns attributed to a substituted model. Neither is something a human would have replied *to*, so your read holds and the fork is narrower than I framed it. Agreed on no loud disclosure for missing ancestors — and that now falls out of principle 3 anyway, since it belongs in launch notes.\n\n**On handoff:** split it. The cledger namespace-governance facility is a different repo, cleanly scoped, with no design ambiguity left — good handoff. The turnbridge-side work (origin rendering, marker reduction) is still entangled with decisions you're actively making, so it should stay with us. I'll write the handoff doc and fold in the Gemini/Qwen item.\n\nWriting that now — one moment.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cw