{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"You said you prefer the axes to the tiers, asked what \"actor identity\" actually refers to, and flagged that this may be a cledger question tied to multi-hop resumption losing data — with context-graph possibly benefiting more than turnbridge. I read cledger and context-graph; new doc is `docs/LINEAGE_AND_COMPOUNDING.md` alongside the first one.\n\n**Actor identity = the person at the keyboard.** `Actor.id` is polymorphic by `actor.type` (`schema.ts:89-95`, \"git author email **or** model id\"): on human turns it's the git email — the human; on agent turns it's the model id — the LLM; the coding CLI is neither, that's `producer.source`. So my \"undisclosed drop\" was about the *human's* email. Solo it's harmless. It bites the collaborative user your PRODUCT_INTENT names: bridge Alice's conversation into Bob's Codex and every human turn renders unattributed, so neither Bob nor the model can tell two people were involved or who asked for what.\n\n**A concrete find while confirming that:** cledger has shipped `producer.model` / `producer.provider` / `producer.source_version` since 0.12.0 explicitly for per-turn agent provenance — and **turnbridge reads none of them**, using `actor.id` everywhere instead. Most pointedly, `opencode.ts:217-221` *guesses* a provider by string-splitting the model id when `producer.provider` is the stated answer (and cledger deliberately leaves it unset rather than inferring, so unset is meaningful). Cheap fix, entirely turnbridge-side.\n\n**On multi-hop: right about the outcome, wrong about the cause — and the real cause is worse.** Lineage *is* stored transitively; `parentOf` is a single-parent map, so walking it reaches the original Claude conversation. Nothing walks it — every consumer is one-hop picker labels (`resume.ts:38-40`). So \"doesn't know enough\" is a consumption gap, not a data gap.\n\nThe actual loss: **turnbridge's degraded output gets re-captured by cledger as canonical evidence, then degraded again on the next hop.** cledger faithfully records the fabricated Codex session because it can't know turnbridge authored it. Four hops in you have `[visible thinking]\\n[visible thinking]\\n…` and a stack of nested import notices. The doc already flags re-bridging, but flagging isn't handling.\n\nYour ecosystem has hit this exact bug class before, and cledger's doc records it as observed while dogfooding turnbridge — the secret-scan reports that \"re-seeded a finding on the report itself, and each scan compounded the last.\" Fixed by **\"Reports carry coordinates, never content.\"** Same disease, and the same family of cure.\n\n**The fix, and why it's smaller than you feared:** context-graph's design already states the principle for this edge (\"reachable through the pointer, not re-materialized\"), but turnbridge *cannot* adopt it — fabrication **is** re-materialization; the target CLI can't follow a pointer into git notes. So the rule changes by one word: **copy from the original, never from the previous copy.** Walk `parentOf` to the root; for any prefix `imported_through_seq` marks as a copy, render from the ancestor's original events. Pure turnbridge change, no schema work, and it also delivers the multi-hop reasoning replay your design doc currently defers as a follow-up — `codex→claude→codex` can restore the real ciphertext because it reads the Codex-origin events, not Claude's prose fold of them.\n\n**What's genuinely cledger's:** not the fix. The real upstream issue is that **conversation-level edges have no owner.** cledger has event→event `links`, `ConversationRef.parent` (sub-conversations only — a bridge is a successor, not a child), and `re_anchor`. No slot for this, so turnbridge improvised a `continuation` kind with the payload in uninterpreted `content` — legitimate under the open-kinds extension point. But context-graph now parses that payload, and intent-recall probably will too. A private convention two other tools depend on is a public interface with no owner or versioning. Promote the `continues` rel + payload shape into cledger's documented vocabulary — graduation, not violation.\n\n**On keeping them divided: the division is sound, the seam is drawn wrong.** It's currently *by phase* (cledger captures, turnbridge resumes), which breaks because turnbridge's output is cledger's input — the pipeline is a **cycle, not a line**, and a phase split can't say who owns a fact created by going around the loop. Seam that survives a cycle: cledger owns *facts about records including how records relate*; turnbridge owns *rendering a record into a target* and, like any adapter, produces facts cledger stores; context-graph owns *derived structure*. That also settles a layering point worth being explicit about — **context-graph can't be where lineage lives**, since it commits to being rebuildable from artifacts, and \"this was fabricated from that\" is not derivable from any artifact. Only turnbridge knows it, only at bridge time. Which is exactly today's arrangement. So you don't need to merge anything.\n\nOpen decisions I'd put back to you rather than assume: whether human attribution *should* transfer into fabricated sessions at all, and whether to record the exact source-event-id list in the `continuation` payload (precise, cheap, no cledger change) versus pursuing event-level `derived_from` in cledger (more general, but capture can't stamp it without cledger learning about turnbridge — an inverted dependency I'd avoid).\n\nresult: Answered all three — actor identity is the human's git email (`schema.ts:89-95`); multi-hop loss is compounding re-capture of turnbridge's own degraded output, not missing lineage, fixable turnbridge-side by rendering copied prefixes from the ancestor's original events; and the only true cledger question is promoting the `continues` conversation-edge vocabulary now that context-graph depends on it. Written to `docs/LINEAGE_AND_COMPOUNDING.md` on `design/policy-split`, plus found that turnbridge ignores cledger's `producer.model`/`provider` provenance fields entirely.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"defab1d48ffb334ef077b543c74110f5a05efd71","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/turnbridge.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","seq":234},"id":"ev1-4785c0290a8fd647b54d93987544f6a04be54b5e26c463dc9f75bef4daea71ac","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-14T01:57:40.985Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"60ec7095-598e-476f-acb7-dd4ee0cab6f1","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.232","tool":"cledger","version":"0.20.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"main","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"text":"You said you prefer the axes to the tiers, asked what \"actor identity\" actually refers to, and flagged that this may be a cledger question tied to multi-hop resumption losing data — with context-graph possibly benefiting more than turnbridge. I read cledger and context-graph; new doc is `docs/LINEAGE_AND_COMPOUNDING.md` alongside the first one.\n\n**Actor identity = the person at the keyboard.** `Actor.id` is polymorphic by `actor.type` (`schema.ts:89-95`, \"git author email **or** model id\"): on human turns it's the git email — the human; on agent turns it's the model id — the LLM; the coding CLI is neither, that's `producer.source`. So my \"undisclosed drop\" was about the *human's* email. Solo it's harmless. It bites the collaborative user your PRODUCT_INTENT names: bridge Alice's conversation into Bob's Codex and every human turn renders unattributed, so neither Bob nor the model can tell two people were involved or who asked for what.\n\n**A concrete find while confirming that:** cledger has shipped `producer.model` / `producer.provider` / `pro